### Day 4 — 2026-09-21 · First vulnerable target + first attack chain (Metasploitable 2, DVWA)

What I did
- Imported Metasploitable 2 (an intentionally vulnerable Linux box) onto the isolated lab bridge (vmbr1) — never on my home network. It only ever lives behind OPNsense on the segmented net.
- Attached its disk as IDE and its NIC as Intel E1000 (not VirtIO), set the boot order to the imported disk, booted it, and logged in (msfadmin).
- Confirmed it pulled a DHCP lease from OPNsense on the lab net (192.168.x.123) — proof it's correctly on vmbr1.
- Recon from Kali: `nmap -sV 192.168.x.123` mapped a large attack surface — FTP, SSH, Telnet, SMB, MySQL, PostgreSQL, Tomcat, IRC, the r-services and more — each with its exact service version.
- Exploit 1 — vsftpd 2.3.4 backdoor, by hand: `nc` to the FTP port, sent a username ending in `:)` to trip the backdoor, then a second `nc` to port 6200 dropped straight into a passwordless root shell.
- Exploit 2 — SQL injection on DVWA (security = Low): confirmed the injection point with a single `'`, dumped every user with `' OR '1'='1`, then pulled credentials with `' UNION SELECT user, password FROM users #`. Extracted the MD5 password hashes and covered how offline cracking works (guess → hash → compare against rockyou.txt).

Challenges & fixes
- Metasploitable's 2008-era kernel has no VirtIO drivers, so a VirtIO disk/NIC wouldn't boot or wouldn't get a link. Fixed by attaching the disk as IDE and the NIC as Intel E1000.
- SSH brute force hit a crypto wall: modern Kali's SSH client has dropped the weak KEX/MAC algorithms that Metasploitable's OpenSSH 4.7 only speaks, so the handshake fails before any password is even tried. Worked around it with explicit legacy options (`-oKexAlgorithms=+diffie-hellman-group1-sha1 -oMACs=+hmac-sha1 -oHostKeyAlgorithms=+ssh-rsa -oCiphers=+aes128-cbc`).
- The noVNC console uses a US keymap, which kept mangling special characters (quotes, pipes, dashes). Used the console Clipboard panel and character-light commands to work around it.

What I learned
- The methodology transfers even when the specific vulnerability is ancient: recon → find a weak point → exploit → prove access is the same everywhere. The CVE changes; the process doesn't.
- The vsftpd backdoor is the textbook ancestor of modern supply-chain attacks (malicious code slipped into an official download) — the same class as SolarWinds and the 2024 XZ Utils backdoor.
- SQL injection's root cause is an app that doesn't separate data from commands: my input got glued straight into the query, so I could break out of the "data" and inject a command. A verbose SQL error is itself an information-disclosure bug. The fix is parameterized queries (prepared statements).
- Hashing: weak passwords behind a fast, unsalted algorithm (MD5) fall in seconds — you don't reverse the hash, you guess-and-compare. Defense is long unique passwords behind a slow, salted hash (bcrypt/argon2).

Verification
- `nmap -sV 192.168.x.123` returned open ports and service versions → the target is live, and only on the lab net.
- vsftpd backdoor: the second `nc` to port 6200 returned `uid=0(root)` and could read /etc/shadow → confirmed full root.
- SQLi: `' OR '1'='1` returned the entire user table; `' UNION SELECT ...` returned usernames plus their password hashes.
- Isolation still holds: the target is reachable from Kali on vmbr1, but not from my home network.

Next
- Switch to the defender side: stand up a Wazuh SIEM and replay today's attacks (backdoor, brute force, SQL injection) to see how they show up in the logs — then tune detection.

